  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts`. It passes `typecheck`, but I never built it with `vite build` or ran it in a browser, so none of it has been looked at on screen. The sun/moon and sky updates, the per-frame `setLight` calls and the water shader are the parts most likely to need tuning.\n\n- **Island:** a jittered-grid heightfield with an irregular coastline, one hill, and sand, grass, rock and peak colours. Every triangle has its own face normal, so it renders faceted. About 16 low-poly pines and 9 jittered rocks sit on it. Everything is one custom mesh with vertex colours.\n- **Water:** a 360-unit grid with a custom shader. Vertex waves are animated by a time uniform. Normals come from screen-space derivatives, so the water looks faceted too. It is turquoise near the island and darker further out, with a fresnel sky reflection, a sun/moon glint, wave-crest foam and a haze to the horizon. The shader reads the day/night uniforms I update each frame.\n- **Day/night:** a 20-second cycle, with the sun above the horizon for about 58% of it.\n  - The `sky.preetham` sun is moved with `setSky`.\n  - The sun light moves, changes colour and fades in and out. It switches off below the horizon, and a moon light takes over.\n  - The hemisphere light dims and goes blue at night, with a minimum intensity so the scene never goes black.\n- **Camera:** an orbit camera about 9.5 units above the water, auto-rotating slowly, with the whole island and some sky in frame.\n- **Contract:** it draws into `#app`. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()` and one animation frame. I avoided top-level `await` because there is no `vite.config.ts` and the default build target would reject it.\n\n",
